WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.690
Speaker 1: This is super, super interesting.

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:05.440
And or at least I found it.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.480
So there was a so there's a mayoral race in
New York City.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:14.539
We're going to talk on the bonus show today
a little bit about Andrew Yang's recent Twitter

00:00:14.539 --> 00:00:15.889
controversy.

00:00:15.889 --> 00:00:20.250
Andrew Yang, as a candidate in the New York
City mayoral race recently, the New York Times

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:23.170
editorial board interviewed each of the candidates.

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:27.589
And this is something that many newspapers
do for presidential races or sometimes Senate

00:00:27.589 --> 00:00:30.009
or whatever the case may be with the mayoral
race.

00:00:30.009 --> 00:00:35.340
The New York Times interviews the candidates
and they ask the candidates, do you know the

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:38.980
median sales price for a home in Brooklyn?

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:46.590
OK, now, I used to live in Brooklyn and at
the time when I last lived there, which was

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:50.770
I guess when was I last in that neighborhood
in 2015.

00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:56.420
If at the time you had asked me the median
home price, I think I would have said 550000

00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:00.200
based on my understanding of of home prices
there.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:06.181
And two of the candidates were asked about
this, Shaun Donovan and Raymond McGuire, and

00:01:06.181 --> 00:01:13.970
they were so far off that it's almost like,
do you have any idea what's going on in the

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:14.970
city?

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:18.460
Shaun Donovan said, I don't know for sure.

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:25.920
I would guess around 100000 dollars, which,
you know, when I see that, I wonder if you're

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:31.380
that out of touch with what's going on with
home prices in Brooklyn.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:37.070
What else that's fundamental to the New York
City experience right now, do you have no

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:38.990
idea about?

00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:43.280
And then Raymond McGuire, who's an investment
banker and former Citigroup executive, was

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:44.280
very similar.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:49.100
He said it's probably between 80 and 90 thousand
dollars, if not higher.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:51.530
Now, I'm no real estate expert.

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:55.380
You know, I'm involved in a number of properties
in different parts of the country.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:02:00.550
And I have, you know, sort of like a passing
knowledge of the fact that in East Coast cities

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:04.659
and and West Coast cities, properties are
really expensive.

00:02:04.659 --> 00:02:10.599
Then you have sort of secondary cities like,
you know, Austin and Asheville, where property

00:02:10.599 --> 00:02:13.879
values are going up, but there's still quite
a bit less.

00:02:13.879 --> 00:02:18.349
And then you've got rural you know, I have
like a passing sense of it.

00:02:18.349 --> 00:02:23.510
How on earth do you get to 80, 90 or 100 thousand
dollar median home price in Brooklyn?

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:27.519
Now, later, one of the candidates said, well,
I thought you meant assessed value.

00:02:27.519 --> 00:02:29.310
It still doesn't make any sense.

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:34.349
Eric Adams, who's the Brooklyn borough president,
said he thought the number was 550.

00:02:34.349 --> 00:02:37.400
That's the number I would have guessed back
in 2015.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:43.930
Maya Wiley, former counsel to Bill de Blasio,
just one point eight million, and Andrew Yang

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:46.269
correctly guessed 900000.

00:02:46.269 --> 00:02:49.049
It's that that that's that's basically right
where we are.

00:02:49.049 --> 00:02:55.029
So how much do you think this is relevant
in thinking about whether a candidate can

00:02:55.029 --> 00:03:02.559
properly manage a city from the standpoint
of if you so misunderstand something as fundamental

00:03:02.559 --> 00:03:09.480
as cost of living, if you underestimate by
eight ninths the cost of a median home in

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:17.109
Brooklyn, what else are you going to be unable
to sort of empathize with or understand?

00:03:17.109 --> 00:03:19.989
To me, it does seem like a real problem.

00:03:19.989 --> 00:03:23.629
Now, I this, by the way, I don't know how
many of you have seen.

00:03:23.629 --> 00:03:27.040
Bill Gates was interviewed by Ellen DeGeneres
a couple of years ago, and they did a sort

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:31.610
of similar thing where Ellen asked Bill Gates,
you know, what do you think, milk costs or

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:32.610
whatever.

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:35.269
And Bill Gates had, like, no idea there was
like some little frozen food thing.

00:03:35.269 --> 00:03:39.150
And Bill Gates thought it cost twenty bucks
when it's like, you know, if if that thing

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:43.040
cost twenty bucks, then, you know, it just
didn't didn't make any sense.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:44.040
Is this relevant?

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:51.840
I think it is relevant, I kind of did a self-test
where I wrote down, what do I think, gasoline,

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:57.860
the average home in Miami, a gallon of milk,
bread and a gallon of eggs cost just to see

00:03:57.860 --> 00:04:01.489
if I had some sense of the sort of like national
numbers.

00:04:01.489 --> 00:04:06.489
So for gasoline, I guess three dollars and
I haven't gotten gas for a while.

00:04:06.489 --> 00:04:09.489
I've had an electric car for for some time
now.

00:04:09.489 --> 00:04:12.379
And gasoline right now is at 301 nationally.

00:04:12.379 --> 00:04:14.609
So that's just like a random guess.

00:04:14.609 --> 00:04:20.079
I seemed sort of clued in to that average
house in Miami, which is a market I sort of

00:04:20.079 --> 00:04:21.680
know, but not intimately.

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:25.919
I guess the median home price in Miami is
three hundred and fifty thousand.

00:04:25.919 --> 00:04:28.240
Real number is three hundred ninety eight
thousand.

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:35.060
So I'm sort of testing whether if you're at
all connected to what's going on in the country,

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:36.060
you would be in the ballpark.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:42.070
And the more I was guessing these numbers,
the more I think guessing 100000 for a Brooklyn

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:47.340
median home price if you're running for mayor
is a major red flag, average price of a gallon

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:48.340
of milk.

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:51.389
Now, I did struggle with this because, first
of all, I never buy a gallon.

00:04:51.389 --> 00:04:55.430
And I know when you buy less, the price per
per per ounce goes up.

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:57.860
I also admittedly, I get hipster milk.

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:00.460
I get like the pasture raised organic.

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:04.229
I pay like five bucks for a half gallon, I
guess for a gallon of organic milk.

00:05:04.229 --> 00:05:05.229
It's five dollars.

00:05:05.229 --> 00:05:07.569
It's actually four.

00:05:07.569 --> 00:05:13.419
And similarly with eggs, ever since reading
Michael Pollan's books about this, I've decided

00:05:13.419 --> 00:05:17.770
the extra couple bucks for pastured eggs and
pastured milk.

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:19.080
I've just decided it's worth it.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:22.270
I don't consume so much that it really makes
a big difference.

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:26.060
So like with eggs, I'm getting those like
bright orange yolk eggs or whatever.

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:27.979
It's like six bucks a dozen.

00:05:27.979 --> 00:05:33.650
So I guess for like your basic cage free eggs,
it's probably something like three dollars

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:35.680
and it turns out it's two sixty six.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:40.280
And then lastly for bread, I struggled with
bread because like, you know, the difference

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:47.449
between a loaf of processed wonder bread and
artisanal seed bread or whatever, it seems

00:05:47.449 --> 00:05:49.710
like it's the difference between a dollar
and eight dollars.

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:51.919
I don't know, I guess three dollars.

00:05:51.919 --> 00:05:54.939
The official rate for bread is two dollars
and fifty cents.

00:05:54.939 --> 00:05:57.210
So, like, how relevant is this stuff?

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:03.930
If you're listening to a candidate say that
they hear they hear you about the issues in

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:07.319
your city, they believe they can fix some
of those issues.

00:06:07.319 --> 00:06:09.540
They want to bring this and they want to bring
that.

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:13.469
And then you ask them, what do you think a
medium home value is when it's nine hundred

00:06:13.469 --> 00:06:16.430
thousand and they say eighty grand?

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:18.740
That seems to me like a material concern.

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:22.090
And I'm curious to hear from you whether you
agree.

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:23.469
Let me know your thoughts.

00:06:23.469 --> 00:06:25.380
You can find me on Twitter at David Pakman.

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:26.990
We will take a quick break.

00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:44.210
And we have so much 

00:06:44.210 --> 00:07:36.449
more upcoming.

